This work introduces a system to predict novel view videos from both temporal and angular perspectives. Unlike prior methods that rely on scene-specific semantic structures for novel view reconstruction, our method utilizes a graph of predicted temporal views to enable prediction across new angular perspectives. Specifically, we first extract views from an input video stream at multiple angles, which are then encoded and stored as nodes within a graph network. A convolutional graph network (CGN) is employed to infer views from unseen angles, utilizing all existing nodes as context. These encoded nodes are processed by a convolutional operation over the graph structure to generate a hidden state, from which the network convolves a local region centered on the node to be predicted at the novel angle. Our approach does not require global structural information, such as point clouds or structural graphs, to anticipate views from novel angles. Experiments conducted on the NTU-RGB+D dataset demonstrate that our method achieves a mean squared error of 0.27 and can predict novel view videos from an maximum angular distance of 45° relative to the input views. Furthermore, the model can be trained on videos captured from a single angle to predict views from other directions. Code is accessible at: https://github.com/rajivkumar1996/NView.

\subsection{Related Work}
Existing methods for novel view video prediction generally fall into two categories: those leveraging explicit structural representations and those utilizing implicit representations. For instance, Li et al. \cite{9030300} and Wu et al. \cite{8956000} employed point clouds to represent scene structure for novel view reconstruction, while Zhang et al. \cite{8899000} and Yang et al. \cite{8870000} used semantic graphs derived from point clouds or skeleton sequences extracted from input views. In contrast, our method predicts novel views without depending on any explicit structural representation. Furthermore, recent approaches like those by Karim et al. \cite{8839000}, Li et al. \cite{8838000}, and Wu et al. \cite{8837000} have attempted novel view prediction using only input views, employing convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and recurrent neural networks (RNNs) on a sequence of views from a fixed angle to guess views from a different angle. However, these methods are limited to a maximum angular difference of 2

